      "historical_entities": []
    },
    {
      "kind": "p",
      "text": "Optical instruments also often use glass prisms whose bases are right isosceles triangles.\nThe arrangements discussed here use ordinary glass prisms in air, for which the critical angle for total internal reflection from glass into air is less than $45^\\circ$.\nUnder this condition, total internal reflection occurs at the relevant reflecting surfaces in the arrangements shown in Figures 161, 162, and 163.\nIn Figure 161, the prism turns the beam through $90^\\circ$, as required in a periscope.\nPrism binoculars use the light path shown in Figure 162, which changes the beam's direction by $180^\\circ$.\nFigure 163 shows how a prism reverses the vertical order of rays emerging from an optical instrument: the upper ray becomes the lower ray, and the lower ray becomes the upper ray.",
      "id": null,
      "label": null,
      "printed_page": 186,
      "source_refs": [
        "p0196#2"
      ],
      "source_text": "在光学仪器中，常常利用底面是等腰直角三角形的玻璃棱镜。它的应用是基于对玻璃的全反射的临界角小于$45^\\circ$。所以在图161、162、163表示的各情况中，都发生全反射。在图161中，棱镜使光束偏转了$90^\\circ$，这在潜望镜中是必需的。在棱镜双目望远镜中发生的是如图162所示的光路的情况：光束的方向变化了$180^\\circ$。图163所示的是利用棱镜改变从某一光学仪器中射出的光线的次序，上面的光线变为下面的，以及反之。",
      "changes": [
        "language",
        "layout",
        "clarification"
      ],
      "numeric_changes": [],
      "historical_entities": []
    },
